\citation{yu2025qpdg}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces One Qiskit program under each dependence rule. (a)~The program; the comment on each line names the operation it creates. (b)~The dependences of $q_0$, the operations where a change can alter $q_0$'s state. (c)--(e)~The slice of $q_0$ that each rule computes (the classical PDG equals def-use here, since the program has no classical bits), with the source lines it keeps and its fidelity at $\lvert 000\rangle $ and at 20 random product inputs. The classical PDG misses the kickback through $o_4$; entanglement closure adds $o_0$, which cannot affect $q_0$; the wire rule keeps exactly the dependences. (f)~State-based pruning of $q_2$ at $\lvert 000\rangle $ is exact at that input but drops three dependences and is wrong at all 20 random inputs. Slices, dependences and verdicts are computed by the study's code from the program in~(a).}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:dependence}{{2}{3}{}{figure.2}{}}
